e sqli of some of  those events  well first of all the utilization of the  defense production act  for  pandemic response is very much like  trying to take a square peg and fit it  in a round hole  so  while i have been amongst the most vocal  proponents of utilizing the dpa  we just have to remember the gpa is a  sort of wartime initiative  to give the president of the united  states the ability to commandeer  industrial resources in order to make  airplanes  right or whatever you need in order to  fight a foreign enemy it was not  designed  uh  to produce  masks or syringes  and so you know now that we are  getting to the point where we can have  some 2020 vision on 2020.  uh we should be thinking about rewriting  a version of the dpa specifically  tailored for health crises one of the  things we found in 2020 was that the  utilization of the gpa was a mess  largely because donald trump was a mess  but he put it in the hands of his  son-in-law who had no experience running  big supply chains but also because it  inherently asks the department of  defense to take a primary role the  department of defense has no experience  in building these kind of supply chains  and as we learned  the agencies that should have that kind  of expertise cdc  nih and hhs also weren't very good at it  so we've got to sort of build a  permanent capacity inside  the federal government to manage medical  supply chains and yes we need to work  with our allies  in order to build the permanent ability  to produce the most important equipment  uh the most important supplies necessary  we i think we've just got to make a  decision that when it comes to certain  items we can't be reliant on a chinese  supply chain that doesn't mean that you  know we have to be  constantly you know making  gaws or syringes it means that we have  to have the the plus up capacity the  surge capacity  in the united states and our ali  structure uh in order to meet that  demand should another pandemic occur  wonderful so so i think there will be um  hopefully a lot of sort of policy  changes uh some introspection and  hopefully some legislation and and  policy interventions uh not all will of  them will be legislative uh  solutions  how do we ensure that any policy changes  that are made to strengthen our pandemic  preparedness  are maintained beyond the current  administration and and the tools that  the  the tools that are developed through  legislation are actually utilized  well it's always a risk in a democracy  uh that a policy adopted by one  government will be reversed by the uh by  the next um but i think if you look at  the areas of alignment and the areas of  of of distinction between the parties  um it gives you a little window forward  into where we can create policy  consistency and where we can't right now  you see the division that exists on mask  and  vaccine mandates um i don't know that  that's going to be solved anytime soon  but there is alignment when it comes to  some of these questions we were just  discussing like supply chain uh i think  there's a there's a growing agreement  inside the republican party that we need  to have an industrial policy as a nation  uh and that belief sort of comes out of  the experience of covet but it also  comes out of the crisis we've seen with  semiconductors um and other critical  items for the industrial supply chain so  the senate back in the summer maybe late  spring early summer passed a bipartisan  bill that for the first time ever really  advanced a u.s industrial policy we  actually allocated billions of dollars  to help subsidize industry in critical  areas so that we can compete with china  so i i think if we do re-evaluate  some of these issues around supply chain  and readiness  we can guarantee some consistency from  administration of administration  wonderful uh so  you were um one of the early supporters  of u.s engagement and funding for the uh  the coalition for epidemic preparedness  innovations and other similar measures  and and for for our sort of audiences  wouldn't uh not everyone would be  familiar with it it's uh it's it was  this entity created  right around the ebola outbreak 2014  2015 outbreak to speed up uh the  availability of vaccines around the  world especially in low middle income  countries and have those vaccines ready  uh for phase three trials after doing  initial development uh for a while until  very recently until the trump admin  until the biden administration uh  they were funders of that entity that  ranged from  ethiopia to norway and britain in  between  but the u.s was  conspicuous by its absence i know that  you have been an early supporter of that  initiative and similar measures  any thoughts on  going forward how do we look at  initiatives like sepi  um or is there anything else that comes  out of this crisis that fills that gap  i think this is a fascinating and really  important debate  how to properly set up incentive  structures to get safe and effective  vaccines for emerging pathogens as  quickly as possible  i as you mentioned have come to the  conclusion that we need to be invested  in international cooperation and sepi is  in my mind the place to do that  the buy administration has invested but  not enough  trump the trump administration sort of  sat on the sidelines let others invest  in seppi the biden administration has  jumped in but not with two feet uh i  think we can do better i don't think  that that um eliminates um our domestic  ability to you know pursue our own  vaccine pathways i i there's some that  say well we shouldn't be in the business  of international cooperation because  you know coronavirus was actually a  example of how nation v nation  competition expedited the development of  vaccines that everybody knew they had to  do it on their own  there was no guarantee you were going to  be saved by somebody else so there was  massive investment you ended up with  european work u.s work chinese russian  uh 