ave to show  that our democracy can rise to meet the  challenges of today which is you know  why the buildback better agenda that  we're discussing right now  um asking the very wealthy to pay their  fair share to fund investments in the  middle of america i think that's  important to our global competition with  china as our efforts to make sure that  everybody in this country has the right  to vote  but you know maybe  [Music]  on the top five list of devastating  trump-era policies was the decision to  withdraw from the who i mean i don't  know that we will recover from that  decision during the first term of the  biden administration because china  having become the biggest player there  in our absence isn't going to give up  that position of hegemony uh easily uh  they are  right now the biggest contributor to  many u.n agencies um the un feels that  china is a more reliable donor  often than  the united states is  so as we try to compete with china's we  try to make sure that u.s health  regulations  u.s and european health standards  become the rules of the road we've got  to be present in a big way in these  multilateral bodies and so this is a  time  and i'm arguing this at the foreign  relations committee for the united  states to double down on our  participation in these organizations  that's one way that we  step up to the plate on china  competition  so as you know that we have had cyclical  interest  a boom and bust cycle of public health  funding in this country  public health departments uh you know we  were very fortunate in connecticut uh  for our state leadership um in terms of  getting the job done with the pandemic  especially the immunization program and  so and so forth but generally speaking  the the capabilities of the health  departments have been eroded  substantially because of these partially  because of these boom and bust cycles  are there any maybe it's too early to  ask that question but has there been any  thinking to get out of that  to use policy levers that add  predictability  to  some of those resources  that health departments specifically but  also federal agencies like the cdc etc  can can expect  i was just having this discussion this  morning in my office with uh kathy  siller who's the ceo of the stanford  hospital health system  she was talking about a really  impactful model that they created and  uh disseminated around stanford to try  to get vaccines into uh low-income and  non-english-speaking communities they  have an extraordinary success rate and  you know to her mind it was and it was a  really important collaboration between  the hospital  public health  the city  local health nonprofits  and you know they were successful in  those low-income neighborhoods and  getting 81 vaccine penetration which is  really  uh remarkable um better than we've seen  in many other uh like neighborhoods  throughout the state and country  but  that's because we funded that effort  right that's because we decided to spend  billions of dollars in relatively  flexible funding so that stanford  hospital could design  uh yale new haven could design  public health programs that  empowered public health professionals to  spend the time that was necessary with  people to get them into the necessary  program in this case the program was  simple a shot in your arm in other cases  the  programs are more complicated whether it  be recovery programs or uh mental health  treatment programs but the tools are the  same public health workers um out there  in the community working outside of a  fee-for-service structure  in a coordinated way  so  your question is whether we've learned  lessons here and i think the answer is  we haven't i mean right now there's not  the kind of talk there should be in  replicating some of the models we use  for vaccine distribution for addiction  for  mental health for obesity  um and that's really  a missed opportunity um  so i'll continue to make that case but i  will say yo